<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<gpx version="1.1" creator="route-planner"
  xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1 http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1/gpx.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <name>Lakeland Way — Anticlockwise, full route with recommended detours</name>
    <desc>Base 208.3km plus 80.5km of peak and swim detours (+5970m ascent), chosen so no day exceeds 30km and the trip averages under 25km.</desc>
  </metadata>
  <wpt lat="54.372438" lon="-3.368438">
    <ele>231.0</ele>
    <name>Muncaster Fell 231m</name>
    <desc>A 231m wooded ridge near the coast at the very start and end of the route. Detour: +3.7km, +171m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.432701" lon="-3.282570">
    <ele>609.0</ele>
    <name>Illgill Head 609m</name>
    <desc>A 609m fell forming the top of the Wast Water Screes, which fall 500m straight into the deepest lake in England. Grassy and gentle from behind, spectacular at the edge. Detour: +4.4km, +325m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.454098" lon="-3.212285">
    <ele>978.0</ele>
    <name>Scafell Pike 978m</name>
    <desc>England&apos;s highest ground at 978m. A boulder-strewn summit plateau with a huge cairn; cairned throughout but genuinely serious in mist, where the plateau defeats casual navigation. Usual approach from the route is via Lingmell Col. Detour: +5.9km, +474m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.466099" lon="-3.180239">
    <ele>785.0</ele>
    <name>Allen Crags 785m</name>
    <desc>A 785m top on the Esk Hause crossing, an easy addition to a Scafell day. Detour: +0.6km, +52m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.447366" lon="-3.165820">
    <ele>902.0</ele>
    <name>Bowfell 902m</name>
    <desc>A 902m pyramid at the head of Langdale with a genuinely rocky top and the Great Slab on its flank. The Band gives a long but never technical ascent. Detour: +3.1km, +336m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.457309" lon="-3.159932">
    <ele>651.0</ele>
    <name>Rossett Pike 651m</name>
    <desc>A 651m top above Rossett Gill and Angle Tarn at the head of Langdale. Detour: +1.4km, +79m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.456888" lon="-3.108992">
    <ele>736.0</ele>
    <name>Harrison Stickle 736m</name>
    <desc>The highest of the Langdale Pikes at 736m, an abrupt rocky top with the whole valley beneath it. Detour: +5.1km, +313m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.457830" lon="-3.110693">
    <ele>700.0</ele>
    <name>Pavey Ark 700m</name>
    <desc>A 700m crag above Stickle Tarn, crossed by Jack&apos;s Rake — a rising Grade 1 scramble up the face of the cliff that is exposed, polished and unforgiving when wet. The summit is reached easily from behind if the Rake is not wanted. Detour: +4.7km, +271m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.502648" lon="-3.210634">
    <ele>697.0</ele>
    <name>Grey Knotts 697m</name>
    <desc>A 697m rocky top directly above Honister Pass. Detour: +1.8km, +141m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.506763" lon="-3.247822">
    <ele>597.0</ele>
    <name>Haystacks 597m</name>
    <desc>At 597m, Wainwright&apos;s favourite fell and where his ashes were scattered. A knot of small tops, tarns and rock — far more interesting than its modest height suggests. Detour: +6.3km, +504m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.587513" lon="-3.261122">
    <ele>770.0</ele>
    <name>Hopegill Head 770m</name>
    <desc>A 770m summit at the head of Hobcarton Crag, one of the sharpest tops here. Detour: +3.1km, +270m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.568648" lon="-3.170826">
    <ele>451.0</ele>
    <name>Cat Bells 451m</name>
    <desc>A 451m ridge above Derwent Water, out of all proportion in popularity to its size — a short, rocky, thoroughly enjoyable up-and-down with a superb view over the lake. Detour: +12.9km, +365m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.593277" lon="-3.032240">
    <ele>726.0</ele>
    <name>Clough Head 726m</name>
    <desc>A 726m fell at the north end of the Dodds with a steep western face. Detour: +2.4km, +372m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.555089" lon="-2.977156">
    <ele>675.0</ele>
    <name>Sheffield Pike 675m</name>
    <desc>A 675m fell between Glencoyne and Glenridding, above old lead workings. Detour: +1.9km, +125m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.515412" lon="-2.877346">
    <ele>696.0</ele>
    <name>Rest Dodd 696m</name>
    <desc>A 696m fell on the Martindale side of the High Street ridge. Detour: +1.6km, +159m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.513781" lon="-2.854141">
    <ele>802.0</ele>
    <name>High Raise (High Street) 802m</name>
    <desc>An 802m broad top, the highest of the far eastern fells after High Street. Detour: +3.0km, +144m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.507436" lon="-2.861731">
    <ele>792.0</ele>
    <name>Rampsgill Head 792m</name>
    <desc>A 792m top with crags falling into Rampsgill, beside Kidsty Pike. Detour: +1.2km, +73m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.505683" lon="-2.855517">
    <ele>780.0</ele>
    <name>Kidsty Pike 780m</name>
    <desc>A 780m top with a neat peaked profile above Riggindale, overlooking Haweswater. Detour: +2.2km, +86m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.482153" lon="-2.878177">
    <ele>784.0</ele>
    <name>Thornthwaite Crag 784m</name>
    <desc>A 784m top marked by a 4m stone beacon, at the junction of several ridges. Detour: +0.8km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.468708" lon="-2.873260">
    <ele>720.0</ele>
    <name>Froswick 720m</name>
    <desc>A 720m cone on the Kentmere horseshoe, a smaller echo of Ill Bell. Detour: +1.2km, +104m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.450290" lon="-3.042519">
    <ele>395.0</ele>
    <name>Silver How 395m</name>
    <desc>A 395m top above Grasmere with a fine view of the vale and the Langdale Pikes. Detour: +2.2km, +213m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.376762" lon="-3.123790">
    <ele>796.0</ele>
    <name>Brim Fell 796m</name>
    <desc>A 796m grassy top on the ridge north of Coniston Old Man. Detour: +5.2km, +450m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.370486" lon="-3.122079">
    <ele>803.0</ele>
    <name>Coniston Old Man 803m</name>
    <desc>An 803m fell rising straight from the village, its flanks full of slate quarries and copper workings. Large summit cairn and a view to the sea. Detour: +5.0km, +507m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.370357" lon="-3.135929">
    <ele>778.0</ele>
    <name>Dow Crag 778m</name>
    <desc>A 778m summit perched above five great climbing buttresses dropping to Goats Water. The walk up is easy; the north face is not. Detour: +3.6km, +181m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.386757" lon="-3.205679">
    <ele>653.0</ele>
    <name>Harter Fell (Eskdale) 653m</name>
    <desc>A 653m fell of rocky tors above Eskdale, and one of the best-shaped small fells in the district. The true summit needs a short easy scramble. Detour: +2.9km, +400m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.410317" lon="-3.186340">
    <ele>549.0</ele>
    <name>Hard Knott 549m</name>
    <desc>A 549m rocky fell above the pass, with the remains of the Roman fort of Mediobogdum on its shoulder. Detour: +2.0km, +142m ascent.</desc>
    <type>peaks</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.428301" lon="-3.259506">
    <ele>254.0</ele>
    <name>Burnmoor Tarn</name>
    <desc>A wide shallow tarn on the moor between Eskdale and Wasdale at 254m. Peaty and comparatively mild; the surrounding ground is boggy, so use the firmer northern shore. Detour: +0.0km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.472261" lon="-3.219753">
    <ele>217.0</ele>
    <name>Emerald Pools</name>
    <desc>Green-tinted pools in the bed of Lingmell Beck below Wasdale Head, where the water has cut into clean rock. Cold and clear; easy entry from the shingle. Detour: +2.6km, +163m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.478084" lon="-3.202674">
    <ele>440.0</ele>
    <name>Styhead Tarn</name>
    <desc>Beneath Great Gable at 440m, right on the pass. Popular by day, quiet at either end of it. Mountain rescue stretcher box nearby. Detour: +1.4km, +49m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.458658" lon="-3.167308">
    <ele>557.0</ele>
    <name>Angle Tarn</name>
    <desc>Sitting at 557m under Bowfell and Rossett Pike, dark and deep with a boulder shore. Reached directly by the route over Rossett Gill. Detour: +0.0km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.522538" lon="-3.165788">
    <ele>167.0</ele>
    <name>Scaleclose Force</name>
    <desc>A fall on the beck below Honister, close to the route. Detour: +1.0km, +45m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.504258" lon="-3.234004">
    <ele>487.0</ele>
    <name>Blackbeck Tarn</name>
    <desc>Just east of Innominate Tarn at 487m, a little larger and usually deserted. Detour: +2.9km, +208m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.505491" lon="-3.240770">
    <ele>528.0</ele>
    <name>Innominate Tarn</name>
    <desc>The tarn on Haystacks where Wainwright&apos;s ashes were scattered, at 528m. Small, peat-bedded and ringed by rock — the view down Ennerdale is the reason to stop. Detour: +3.4km, +261m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.502068" lon="-2.879572">
    <ele>428.0</ele>
    <name>Hayeswater</name>
    <desc>A former reservoir at 428m under Gray Crag, now returned to a natural tarn. Detour: +0.3km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.488920" lon="-2.852351">
    <ele>486.0</ele>
    <name>Blea Water</name>
    <desc>The deepest tarn in England at around 63m, held in a textbook glacial corrie under High Street at 486m. Very cold, very clear, with a boulder shore and no easy shallows. Detour: +3.9km, +341m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.452227" lon="-2.979696">
    <ele>146.0</ele>
    <name>High Fall</name>
    <desc>A fall in the Rydal Beck valley behind Rydal Hall. Detour: +0.7km, +35m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.427755" lon="-3.029350">
    <ele>56.0</ele>
    <name>Elter Water</name>
    <desc>Shallow and reed-fringed at 56m, with the Langdale Pikes filling the view. Warmest water on the route by some margin. Detour: +2.1km, +13m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.419693" lon="-3.066548">
    <ele>104.0</ele>
    <name>Little Langdale Tarn</name>
    <desc>A quiet valley tarn at 104m with a reedy shore; much less visited than Elter Water. Detour: +2.3km, +64m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.384129" lon="-3.111418">
    <ele>413.0</ele>
    <name>Levers Water</name>
    <desc>A dammed tarn at 413m in the Coppermines valley above Coniston, surrounded by mine workings. Deep, clear and cold. Detour: +0.8km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.374549" lon="-3.117781">
    <ele>546.0</ele>
    <name>Low Water</name>
    <desc>A shelf tarn at 546m directly below the summit of Coniston Old Man. Detour: +3.3km, +260m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.360667" lon="-3.136620">
    <ele>565.0</ele>
    <name>Blind Tarn</name>
    <desc>A true corrie tarn at 565m on Dow Crag with no inlet or outlet stream — it is fed entirely by the fell around it. Detour: +0.9km, +54m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="54.361363" lon="-3.297380">
    <ele>234.0</ele>
    <name>Devoke Water</name>
    <desc>The largest tarn in the district at 234m, alone on Birker Fell with no fells immediately around it. Big skies, no shelter, rarely anyone there. Detour: +3.3km, +0m ascent.</desc>
    <type>wild-swim-spots</type>
  </wpt>
  <trk>
    <name>Lakeland Way (with detours)</name>
